"""Which of the JavaScript builtins shipped inside JavaScriptCore a test run executed.

generate-coverage-report measures compiled code, so it says nothing about
Source/JavaScriptCore/builtins -- 102 functions of shipping JavaScript that implement Array
iteration, Promise, async-from-sync, the iterator helpers and the Inspector's injected script.
This measures them, using JSC's own control-flow profiler:

    Tools/Scripts/generate-javascript-coverage JSTests/stress/array-prototype-forEach.js
    Tools/Scripts/generate-javascript-coverage JSTests/stress            # a directory
    Tools/Scripts/generate-javascript-coverage --jobs 8 JSTests --json /tmp/js.json

With no test paths it measures what starting jsc and evaluating nothing executes, which is a
real and instant measurement and is labelled as such.

This tool sees only what jsc can run. For the JavaScript that only runs inside a browser -- the
Web Inspector front end, WebCore's injected scripts, page JavaScript -- use
generate-inspector-js-coverage, which drives the same control-flow profiler over the Web Inspector
protocol.

The headline is the list of builtins nothing called, by file, function and line. Every number
is a lower bound and printed with a >= for the reason Tools/CodeCoverage/README.md gives: this
run is necessarily a subset, because layout tests reach builtins that jsc alone does not.

HOW, in one paragraph. Each run is `jsc --useControlFlowProfiler=true --useDollarVM=true
<tests> <driver>`, where the driver calls $vm.dumpBasicBlockExecutionRanges() and jsc prints
every basic block of every JS source it compiled, with its text range and execution count. All
102 builtins live in one concatenated 140,940-byte source (s_JSCCombinedCode), so the offsets
are turned back into files and lines using the build's own generated JSCBuiltins.cpp. See
webkitpy/coverage_javascript.py for the offset mapping and for how the builtins source is
identified in the dump.

TWO THINGS TO KNOW.

This does not build anything and cannot: it needs a build whose jsc exists, and unlike the
native pipeline it does NOT need that build to be instrumented -- the control-flow profiler is a
Normal option present in every configuration. Pointing it at an ordinary Release build should
work and be faster; that follows from OptionsList.h rather than from measurement, since the
checkout this was written in has no non-coverage jsc to try.

It writes no profiles into /private/tmp/WebKitCoverage. An instrumented jsc has that path baked
into it and the directory is machine-global, so running jsc five thousand times would land five
thousand raw profiles in the middle of whoever's native coverage run is in flight. Every jsc
this tool starts has LLVM_PROFILE_FILE pointed at a directory of its own, or at /dev/null.
"""

import json
import logging
import optparse
import os
import shutil
import sys
import tempfile
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor

from webkitpy.common.system.filesystem import FileSystem
from webkitpy.common.webkit_finder import WebKitFinder
from webkitpy.coverage_javascript import (
    JSC_PROFILER_OPTIONS, BuiltinsCoverage, BuiltinsIndex, JavaScriptCoverageReport, JSCDriver,
    expand_test_paths, find_generated_builtins, find_jsc, format_report)
from webkitpy.coverage_scope import CoverageScope

_log = logging.getLogger('generate-javascript-coverage')

# Build directories to look in, relative to the checkout, in preference order. The coverage
# tree first because that is the one Tools/CodeCoverage's recipes produce and the one somebody
# running this alongside a native coverage run already has; a plain Release build works just as
# well and is second because it might be a different revision from the coverage tree.
_BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES = (
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'cmake-mac', 'Coverage'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild-Coverage', 'Release'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'Release'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'cmake-mac', 'Release'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'Debug'),
)

_EPILOG = """
WHAT THE NUMBERS MEAN

Functions is the number that has a complete denominator. Every builtin the build embedded is in
the index whether it ran or not, because the index comes from the generated table and not from
the dump, so "94 of 102" counts all of them. A builtin nothing called has NO basic blocks at
all -- JSC generates a builtin's bytecode on first call, and the profiler registers its blocks
during bytecode generation -- so zero blocks is the strongest possible statement that it never
ran, not missing data.

Lines is per basic block, over the statement lines of each builtin: the lines between the
`function` keyword and the closing brace that are not blank, not comment-only and not
punctuation-only. Braces are excluded for the reason Tools/CodeCoverage/README.md gives for the
native pipeline -- a function ending in an explicit `return` never executes the bytecode at its
closing brace, so counting `}` would report an uncovered line in every builtin that is fully
exercised. A line is covered when a block that overlaps a non-whitespace character of it
executed; a block that reaches only into the next line's indentation does not cover that line.

Both are LOWER BOUNDS, and printed with >=. jsc is not the only thing that runs these builtins:
Promise, the module loader and the iterator protocol are exercised far harder by the layout
tests than by anything here, and a builtin this reports as never executed may only be one this
subset never reached. Six of the eight that the whole stress suite never runs are Web Inspector
code that jsc cannot reach at all; see Tools/CodeCoverage/JavaScriptCoverage.md.

WHEN A RUN IS NOT COUNTED

jsc evaluates every file it is given into one global object, in order, and the driver is last.
A test that calls quit() therefore ends the process before the driver runs, and that run
contributes nothing -- it is counted and named under "runs produced no attributable dump"
rather than silently dropped. Measured over JSTests/stress that is 82 of 5,926 runs. A test that
throws does not have this problem: jsc reports the exception and carries on to the next file.

--batch-size trades that risk for speed. With N tests per jsc process the fixed 40ms of startup
is amortised, and one quit() costs the whole batch instead of one file. The default is 1.
"""


class _PreformattedEpilog(optparse.IndentedHelpFormatter):
    def format_epilog(self, epilog):
        return epilog or ''


def parse_args(args):
    parser = optparse.OptionParser(
        usage='%prog [options] [TEST_OR_DIRECTORY ...]', epilog=_EPILOG,
        formatter=_PreformattedEpilog())
    parser.add_option('--build-directory',
                      help='Build directory holding jsc and its generated builtins table. '
                           'Defaults to the first of {} that has both'.format(
                               ', '.join(_BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES)))
    parser.add_option('--jsc', help='Path to the jsc binary, if not the build directory\'s')
    parser.add_option('--generated-builtins',
                      help='Path to the build\'s JSCBuiltins.cpp, if not the build '
                           'directory\'s. This is where the offset-to-file mapping comes from, '
                           'so it must be the one the jsc being run was built from')
    parser.add_option('--jsc-option', action='append', dest='jsc_options', default=[],
                      metavar='OPTION',
                      help='An extra option to pass to every jsc process, e.g. '
                           '--jsc-option=--useShadowRealm=1. Repeatable. Needed because this '
                           'tool does not read the //@ runDefault() directives at the top of a '
                           'JSTests file, so a feature a test enables for itself is off here: '
                           'all four ShadowRealmPrototype.js builtins read 0% over the whole '
                           'stress suite until --useShadowRealm=1 is passed')
    parser.add_option('--jobs', type='int', default=max(1, (os.cpu_count() or 4) // 2),
                      metavar='N', help='jsc processes to run at once (default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('--batch-size', type='int', default=1, metavar='N',
                      help='Test files per jsc process (default: %default). See the note below')
    parser.add_option('--timeout', type='int', default=120, metavar='SECONDS',
                      help='Kill a jsc process after this long (default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('--dump', action='append', default=[], metavar='PATH',
                      help='Read an already-captured jsc dump instead of running anything. A '
                           'file, or a directory of them. Repeatable, and they accumulate the '
                           'same way separate runs do')
    parser.add_option('--save-dumps', metavar='DIR',
                      help='Write each run\'s stderr into DIR, so a long run can be re-reported '
                           'with --dump without running jsc again')
    parser.add_option('--profile-dir', metavar='DIR',
                      help='Where an instrumented jsc should write its .profraw files. Defaults '
                           'to a temporary directory, never to the baked-in machine-global one')
    parser.add_option('--json', metavar='PATH', help='Also write the report as JSON')
    parser.add_option('--no-uncovered-lines', action='store_false', dest='uncovered_lines',
                      default=True,
                      help='Omit the per-builtin uncovered-line detail, leaving the summary and '
                           'the never-executed list')
    parser.add_option('-v', '--verbose', action='store_true', default=False,
                      help='Verbose logging')
    options, remaining = parser.parse_args(args)
    if options.jobs < 1:
        parser.error('--jobs must be at least 1')
    if options.batch_size < 1:
        parser.error('--batch-size must be at least 1')
    return options, remaining


def resolve_build_directory(options, checkout_root):
    """The build directory, and the jsc and builtins table inside it.

    Both have to come from the same build. A jsc from one tree and a JSCBuiltins.cpp from
    another would place every offset in the wrong builtin without any symptom at all, so a
    mismatch is worth an explicit warning: the generated file is compared against the binary by
    mtime, which is weak but is the only thing on disk that relates them.
    """
    candidates = [options.build_directory] if options.build_directory else [
        os.path.join(checkout_root, relative) for relative in _BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES]
    for candidate in candidates:
        jsc = options.jsc or find_jsc(candidate)
        builtins = options.generated_builtins or find_generated_builtins(candidate)
        if jsc and builtins:
            return candidate, jsc, builtins
    return None, options.jsc, options.generated_builtins


def warn_if_stale(jsc_path, generated_builtins):
    try:
        if os.path.getmtime(generated_builtins) > os.path.getmtime(jsc_path):
            _log.warning('%s is newer than %s, so the offsets in the dump may belong to a '
                         'different set of builtins than the ones this places',
                         os.path.basename(generated_builtins), os.path.basename(jsc_path))
    except OSError:
        pass


def batches(paths, size):
    for start in range(0, len(paths), size):
        yield paths[start:start + size]


def read_dumps(paths):
    """[(label, text)] from files and directories of saved jsc stderr."""
    dumps = []
    for path in paths:
        if os.path.isdir(path):
            for name in sorted(os.listdir(path)):
                candidate = os.path.join(path, name)
                if os.path.isfile(candidate):
                    dumps.append((candidate, _read_text(candidate)))
        else:
            dumps.append((path, _read_text(path)))
    return dumps


def _read_text(path):
    with open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as handle:
        return handle.read()


def run_tests(driver, test_batches, driver_path, options, checkout_root):
    """[(label, dump text)] for every batch, run at most options.jobs at a time.

    Only the subprocesses are concurrent; the merge is done by the caller on one thread, since
    BuiltinsCoverage accumulates into one dict and the merge is microseconds against jsc's 40ms.
    """
    results = []
    total = len(test_batches)

    def one(batch):
        # An empty batch is the "no test paths" case: jsc runs the driver and nothing else.
        label = os.path.relpath(batch[0], checkout_root) if batch else '(jsc startup)'
        if len(batch) > 1:
            label += ' +{}'.format(len(batch) - 1)
        returncode, stderr = driver.run(batch, driver_path, timeout=options.timeout)
        return label, returncode, stderr

    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=options.jobs) as pool:
        for index, (label, returncode, stderr) in enumerate(pool.map(one, test_batches), 1):
            if returncode is None:
                _log.warning('%s timed out after %ds', label, options.timeout)
            if options.save_dumps:
                name = label.replace(os.sep, '_').replace(' ', '_') + '.dump'
                with open(os.path.join(options.save_dumps, name), 'w') as handle:
                    handle.write(stderr)
            results.append((label, stderr))
            if index % 250 == 0 or index == total:
                _log.info('  %d/%d', index, total)
    return results


def main(args):
    options, test_arguments = parse_args(args)
    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG if options.verbose else logging.INFO,
                        format='%(message)s')

    checkout_root = WebKitFinder(FileSystem()).webkit_base()
    build_directory, jsc_path, generated_builtins = resolve_build_directory(options,
                                                                           checkout_root)
    if not generated_builtins:
        _log.error('Found no generated JSCBuiltins.cpp. Looked in %s. Pass --build-directory '
                   'or --generated-builtins.',
                   ', '.join(_BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES))
        return 1
    if not options.dump and not jsc_path:
        _log.error('Found no jsc to run. Looked in %s. Pass --build-directory, --jsc, or '
                   '--dump to report on an already-captured dump.',
                   ', '.join(_BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES))
        return 1

    try:
        index = BuiltinsIndex.load(checkout_root, generated_builtins)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as failure:
        _log.error('Could not read the builtins table from %s: %s', generated_builtins, failure)
        return 1
    placed, verified, total = index.verification_summary()
    _log.info('Indexed %d builtins from %s: %d placed in a .js file, %d with a verified line '
              'mapping', total, os.path.relpath(generated_builtins, checkout_root), placed,
              verified)
    if not total:
        _log.error('The builtins table is empty, so there is nothing to report on')
        return 1

    coverage = BuiltinsCoverage(index)
    test_paths = expand_test_paths(test_arguments)
    # Checked here rather than left to jsc, because jsc's response to a path that does not
    # exist is to report it and exit before the driver, which arrives as "the dump could not be
    # attributed" -- a true statement about the wrong problem.
    missing = [path for path in test_paths if not os.path.isfile(path)]
    if missing:
        _log.error('These test paths do not exist: %s', ', '.join(missing[:10]))
        return 1
    scope_argv = test_arguments or ['(jsc startup only)']

    if options.dump:
        if jsc_path:
            warn_if_stale(jsc_path, generated_builtins)
        dumps = read_dumps(options.dump)
        _log.info('Reading %d saved dump(s)', len(dumps))
        for label, text in dumps:
            coverage.add_dump(text, label=label)
        test_count = len(dumps)
        scope_argv = list(options.dump)
    else:
        warn_if_stale(jsc_path, generated_builtins)
        if options.save_dumps:
            os.makedirs(options.save_dumps, exist_ok=True)
        temporary = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='webkit-js-coverage-')
        try:
            profile_directory = options.profile_dir or os.path.join(temporary, 'profiles')
            os.makedirs(profile_directory, exist_ok=True)
            driver = JSCDriver(jsc_path,
                               framework_path=build_directory or os.path.dirname(jsc_path),
                               profile_directory=profile_directory,
                               combined_source_length=index.combined_length,
                               options=JSC_PROFILER_OPTIONS + tuple(options.jsc_options))
            driver_path = os.path.join(temporary, 'webkit-js-coverage-driver.js')
            with open(driver_path, 'w') as handle:
                handle.write(driver.driver_source())

            # An empty batch is how "no test paths" is expressed: jsc still runs the driver,
            # and the answer is what starting a VM executes. Worth having as a baseline for
            # everything else, since those builtins are covered by every run.
            test_batches = list(batches(test_paths, options.batch_size)) or [[]]
            _log.info('Running %s over %d test file(s) in %d process(es), %d at a time',
                      os.path.relpath(jsc_path, checkout_root), len(test_paths),
                      len(test_batches), options.jobs)
            _log.debug('Profiles from this run go to %s, not to the baked-in path',
                       profile_directory)
            results = run_tests(driver, test_batches, driver_path, options, checkout_root)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(temporary, ignore_errors=True)
        for label, text in results:
            coverage.add_dump(text, label=label or '(jsc startup)')
        test_count = len(test_paths)

    scope = CoverageScope.selective(scope_argv, tests_run=coverage.runs_attributed,
                                   suite_name='jsc')
    report = JavaScriptCoverageReport(coverage, jsc_path=jsc_path, test_count=test_count,
                                      scope=scope)

    for line in format_report(report, show_uncovered_lines=options.uncovered_lines):
        print(line)
    for line in scope.banner_lines():
        print(line)

    if options.json:
        with open(options.json, 'w') as handle:
            json.dump(report.to_json(), handle, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
            handle.write('\n')
        _log.info('Wrote %s', options.json)

    if not coverage.runs_attributed:
        _log.error('No run produced a dump that could be attributed to the builtins source, so '
                   'every number above is zero for want of data rather than for want of '
                   'coverage')
        return 1
    return 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
